  :adr/title "CLI Bots use the explicit Ornith edge route"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-29"
  :adr/body
  "## The request

`itonami` CLI の Bots を `murakumo.cloud` の Ornith model に接続し、16 GB
Mac mini で安定運用できるかを実生成で検証する。

## Before

The resident installation was admitted to the Murakumo provider, but its
workforce override and every observed Bot still selected `murakumo-main`.
The 24-hour Bot SLO at 08:46Z was failing: 167 turns, 18 completed, 146 failed,
10.8% completion, 70.1% provider timeouts, p50 214 s and p90 1,203 s. This is a
historical window over `murakumo-main`; it is the baseline, not an Ornith result.

## Decision

Resident workforce Bots select the literal `murakumo-edge` route. It is added
to the shipped Murakumo catalog with the 65,536-token window advertised by the
live `/v1/models` endpoint. There is deliberately no fallback from
`murakumo-edge` to `murakumo-main`: a fallback could make an edge stability
test succeed on GAD or B70 and therefore answer the wrong question.

The provider-wide default stays `murakumo-main`. This decision changes Bots,
not unrelated interactive model calls. A per-Bot `itonami bots model` choice
still wins, and provisioning can still read an explicit role profile or an
operator workforce override.

## Live verification, 2026-08-29 08:47-09:05Z

The path under test was the real one:

    itonami CLI -> resident server -> api.murakumo.cloud -> murakumo-edge

The shell held no `MURAKUMO_API_KEY`; the resident process retained the
credential boundary. A dedicated Bot was changed with `itonami bots model`,
then asked for nonce-only answers without tools or external effects.

- First generation: exact `ORNITH_EDGE_OK_20260829`; model
  `murakumo-edge`; inference turn 32.6 s; fresh CLI process end-to-end 49.4 s.
- Sequential soak in one CLI process: 10/10 exact answers, 0 timeout, 0 HTTP
  error and 0 model mismatch. End-to-end p50 34.84 s, p95/max 37.51 s.
- Before the soak, the public inference registry reported ten
  `murakumo-edge` workers; 10/10 were enrolled, live, ready and fresh.
- After the soak, the same ten were still enrolled/live/ready/fresh with ten
  free slots. Fleet telemetry reported ten 16 GiB macOS workers, all online
  and reachable. Memory in use was 4.34-13.48 GiB (median 10.65 GiB); the
  newest telemetry sample was under 10 s old.

The application suite after the configuration change ran 2,064 tests / 12,144
assertions with 0 failures and 0 errors.

## Landed and resident, 2026-08-29 09:09-09:17Z

PR #221 merged as `67041af073329aeea7f7ebf8df451d57036b4b51`. The
LaunchAgent `dev.cloud-itonami.app` was moved from immutable release `4a2dbb3`
to the matching `67041af` release; the old directory and the existing store
were retained for rollback. `/health` returned the resident store fingerprint,
and the PATH-resolved `itonami status` reported `serves-this-store?: true`.

Re-provisioning moved all 92 workforce Bots through the configuration path.
Six remaining saved Murakumo Bots were updated through `itonami bots model`.
The post-change inventory was 99 enabled Murakumo Bots, 99 on
`murakumo-edge`, zero on another model. A post-release real generation returned
exact `ORNITH_RELEASE_67041AF_OK` in 31.35 s. The public registry still reported
ten of ten Ornith workers live/ready/fresh with all ten slots free afterwards.

## Stabilization integration, 2026-08-29

Two real resident goal runs then exposed a different boundary: generation was
healthy, but Ornith kept describing completion in prose for 11/13 turns and
never called the host's completion tool. A direct request to the same live
worker with OpenAI `tool_choice: required` returned an exact
`goal_complete({summary, evidence})` tool call. The app therefore uses the
wire-level required-tool contract for tool-bearing `murakumo-edge` goals and
fails the turn immediately as `:provider/required-tool-missing` if a provider
violates it. This replaces an unproductive 24-turn prose loop; it does not mark
the goal successful without evidence.

Prefix reuse is integrated as a soft placement contract across the three
components rather than an application result cache:

1. Cloud Itonami hashes the conversation id and sends only the 64-hex digest as
   `x-murakumo-affinity-key`; the raw Bot/conversation identity does not cross
   the provider boundary.
2. local-murakumo chooses a stable live Ornith worker for that digest and gives
   it an eight-second exclusive preference. This is deliberately distinct from
   AIUEOS `target-did`: a hard physical-device target never falls back, while a
   cache preference expires so a busy or dead Mac cannot pin the request.
3. Each Murakumo poll worker includes its DID when listing queue work, while
   claim remains the authoritative atomic check.

The bounded edge response now preserves measured llama.cpp
`prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` and bounded timing fields, and adds the
serving worker DID. Cloud Itonami accumulates cached tokens without turning an
unmeasured value into zero and projects the serving DID on the public turn.
This makes same-worker cache reuse and fallback observable per task.

## Post-integration evidence and production blocker, 2026-08-29 12:16-12:18Z

The stabilization source is merged in all three owners: Murakumo
`590de39a` (worker DID on queue polling), local-murakumo `64584add` (soft
affinity plus a ten-minute queue hot window and five-minute queue shards), and
Cloud Itonami `09efa016` (hashed affinity, required-tool fail-fast, cache and
serving-worker evidence). The resident app runs the immutable `09efa016`
release and `/health` is green. The Murakumo poll-worker source has SHA-256
`b414ad4df215a229c831878b11648c48ba6666fecbf47bcc9228f873be533c07`
on all ten canonical 16 GiB Mac minis; all ten then appeared simultaneously as
live, ready, and fresh in the public registry.

The bounded Worker tree is merged, builds successfully, and was uploaded as
Cloudflare Version `479d1c89-1a6a-4ea8-ac2e-11b4f2fb4d8c` with zero
production traffic. It is therefore a staged artifact, not a live release.
The production deployment still runs an older queue implementation. A public
required-tool request at 12:16Z again returned HTTP 503 / Cloudflare error
1102 after about 37 seconds. This failure occurred before a resident model
could complete the request and is not an Ornith tool-call result.

The production switch was deliberately stopped by the local deployment safety
gate: the shared root checkout is behind `origin/main` and has overlapping
uncommitted owner work, so synchronizing or releasing from it could roll back
unrelated production changes. The existing sovereign CDCI allowlist contains
test, build, and smoke actions but no production-release action; it was not
misrepresented as a deployment path. Until the staged Version receives
production traffic and the goal/cache tests pass, task completion rate and
cache-hit effectiveness remain unqualified.

## Qualification boundary

The original sequential soak established short-generation health and memory
headroom, but did not prove per-request worker attribution or goal completion.
The integrated fields now make attribution measurable, but a
post-release goal/tool run and repeated-prefix cache observation are still
required before calling the stabilization live. The honest production gate
remains the existing Bot SLO after enough new Ornith turns enter its window:
completion >=90%, provider timeout <2%, stale runs 0, with the model recorded
as `murakumo-edge`.
"}]
